# Mesh Node Configuration
# Copy this to .env and fill in your values:  cp .env.example .env
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THIS FILE IS FOR CONFIGURATION. SECRETS GO IN secrets/env.sops.
#
# `.env` holds non-sensitive configuration only — node identity, URLs,
# ports, feature flags, model names. Every credential (passwords, API
# keys, tokens, passphrases) belongs in the SOPS store instead.
#
# Why: a credential kept in plaintext here AND in env.sops defeats the point
# of SOPS ("nothing sensitive at rest") and gives the credential two sources
# that can silently drift.
#
# How secrets reach the containers: the launcher scripts source
# scripts/lib-secrets.sh -> load_secrets, which decrypts env.sops and
# EXPORTS each value; compose then interpolates ${VAR} from that
# environment. So `noo stack start` is the required entry point — a bare
# `docker compose up` resolves those secrets to EMPTY (verified: with the
# launcher POSTGRES_PASSWORD renders a real value, without it `""`).
#
# Adding or rotating a secret — decrypt, edit, re-encrypt. BOTH type
# flags are required; the file is dotenv but named `.sops`, so sops
# otherwise guesses JSON and silently emits nothing:
#   sops -d --input-type dotenv --output-type dotenv secrets/env.sops > /tmp/e
#   $EDITOR /tmp/e
#   cp /tmp/e secrets/env.new.sops   # path must match the .sops.yaml rule
#   sops -e -i --input-type dotenv --output-type dotenv secrets/env.new.sops
#   mv secrets/env.new.sops secrets/env.sops && shred -u /tmp/e
# Then verify NOTHING was dropped before trusting it — a re-encrypt that
# loses keys is silent:
#   sops -d --input-type dotenv --output-type dotenv secrets/env.sops \
#     | grep -cE '^[A-Z_]+='
#
# See docs/security/secrets.md.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Node identity
NODE_ID=my-node
NODE_IP=192.168.1.100
CLUSTER_MODE=controller
NODE_TIER=1
GPU_MODEL=
VRAM_GB=0
CONTROLLER_URL=https://gateway:8080
# The gateway serves HTTPS ONLY: it refuses to start unless NOO_TLS_CERT,
# NOO_TLS_KEY and NOO_TLS_CA (written by `noo onboard --only enrollment`)
# all name readable files. There is no plaintext listener and no opt-out.
IS_ARM=false

# Storage
NOO_DATA_ROOT=/mnt/d/pai

# Backend settings
# vLLM model default lives in noomesh.yaml (cluster.backends.vllm.model);
# compose carries the same fallback. Set here only to override per-site:
# VLLM_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3-8B-AWQ
GPU_MEM_UTIL=0.9
LLAMACPP_MODEL=/models/llama-7b-Q5_K_M.gguf
N_GPU_LAYERS=32
N_CTX=4096
AIRLLM_MODEL=meta-llama/Llama-2-70b-hf
MAX_VRAM_GB=8.0

# Monitoring
#
# GRAFANA_PASSWORD has no value here ON PURPOSE. A placeholder such as
# `changeme` is worse than blank: the launcher's guard only refuses an EMPTY
# value, so a copied .env.example would start a Grafana whose admin login was
# a word in this file, and every later scan would see a "set" password.
# `noo onboard` mints a real one into the secret store. Setting it by hand:
#   noo security generate-secret | head -c 32   (then `noo secrets set`)
# Grafana copies it into its own DB on FIRST boot only — changing it later
# means changing it in Grafana too (see docs/security/secrets.md).
GRAFANA_PASSWORD=

# Grafana's door: where its port is published and the URL the
# BROWSER uses. `noo onboard` writes both from NOO_BIND_MODE — loopback and
# https://localhost:3000 (a browser on the controller, or through an SSH
# tunnel), or https://<controller-tailnet-name>:3000 on a tailnet controller,
# where `tailscale serve` fronts the port with the tailnet's certificate and
# Grafana moves to loopback once that is confirmed (the tailnet IP until
# then) — and `noo upgrade --stack` fills an empty GRAFANA_ROOT_URL. Empty = Grafana
# single sign-on is off ("Open Grafana" answers 503). Never NOO_BIND_ADDR's
# 0.0.0.0: `all` does not put Grafana on every interface.
NOO_GRAFANA_BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
GRAFANA_ROOT_URL=
# Where a bookmark of Grafana's tailnet door is sent: the console's
# https://<tailnet-name>/#/observability?open=grafana. `noo onboard` and
# `noo upgrade --stack` write it on a tailnet controller (and map /login on
# Grafana's port to it via tailscale serve). Empty = a bookmark shows
# Grafana's bare welcome page.
NOO_GRAFANA_CONSOLE_URL=
# Grafana session length in minutes (1–60). A disabled noomesh account's
# open Grafana tab lasts at most this long.
NOO_GRAFANA_SESSION_TTL=15

# API keys (required for respective features)
HF_TOKEN=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Channels (optional). Inbound webhooks run through one generic path (the
# Python ChannelAdapter verifies/parses). A channel is enabled simply by configuring its credentials
# below — the adapter then registers and its /api/channels/<name>/webhook URL
# accepts inbound requests. A channel with no creds has no adapter and its
# webhook returns 404. There is no separate enable flag.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Who may talk to the bot is `noo_user_channel_links`:
#   noo security user link <username> telegram <numeric id>
# (TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS is retired: it admits nobody, compose no longer
# delivers it, and the launcher warns while .env still carries it.)
# Telegram ingestion mode. Leave TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL EMPTY for long-polling
# (getUpdates) — no public URL, no inbound ingress, fully private (the bot
# dials out to Telegram). Set it to a public HTTPS base URL only if you want
# push webhooks instead (then that URL must be internet-reachable, e.g. via a
# tunnel). Empty is the private default.
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL=
# Public base URL for the node-bootstrap script when nodes cannot reach the
# controller directly. NOT a credential and NOT ngrok-specific — any public
# hostname works. (There is no ngrok sidecar and no
# NGROK_AUTHTOKEN; Google Chat pulls from Pub/Sub and Telegram
# long-polls, so no public webhook ingress is needed at all.)
NGROK_DOMAIN=
GOOGLE_CHAT_KEY_FILE=
GOOGLE_CHAT_CREDENTIALS=
# Google Chat ingestion mode. EMPTY = HTTP webhook (needs a public URL). Set to
# a full Cloud Pub/Sub subscription (projects/<project>/subscriptions/<name>)
# to PULL events instead — no public ingress, fully private. The SA needs
# roles/pubsub.subscriber on it, and the Chat app's connection setting must
# point at the matching topic (roles/pubsub.publisher for chat-api-push@).
GOOGLE_CHAT_PUBSUB_SUBSCRIPTION=
# GCP project id/number (used for webhook token verification and as a default
# for the Pub/Sub project when the subscription is given as a bare name).
GOOGLE_CHAT_PROJECT_ID=

# Third-party image pins. Compose defaults are pinned (nats by
# digest, vllm to v0.25.0) so `:latest` can't drift under you. Override to bump
# deliberately. ollama is left overridable — pin it to a verified version/digest
# before relying on it. CI runs a Trivy scan gate on every PR.
# NATS_IMAGE=nats:latest@sha256:7ff2e641c33dbdb0dd13a943ba0b70d841b71275c607baa53ef9b8661f0b2d8c
# VLLM_IMAGE=vllm/vllm-openai:v0.25.0
# OLLAMA_IMAGE=ollama/ollama:0.x   # pin to a verified version

# Model governance (US-vendor focus). The model catalog only surfaces/places
# models whose FAMILY is an approved vendor AND whose TASK is approved.
#   NOO_MODEL_VENDORS   families to allow (default: gemma-4,phi,harrier,nemotron,minitron,llama
#                       = Google/Microsoft/Nvidia/Meta). Comma-separated.
#   NOO_MODEL_TASKS     HF pipeline tags to allow (default: text-generation,
#                       feature-extraction,sentence-similarity).
#   NOO_MODEL_FILTER_MODE  strict (default, approved-only) | prefer | off
NOO_MODEL_VENDORS=
NOO_MODEL_TASKS=
NOO_MODEL_FILTER_MODE=strict

# ── Embedding (topology is documented in noomesh.yaml
# `services.embedding_*` — env values here OVERRIDE it) ──
# ALL embedding traffic (external proxy, memory search, trellion ingest)
# goes through one backend-agnostic client at PAGEINDEX_EMBEDDING_URL.
# PAGEINDEX_EMBEDDING_BACKEND picks the wire format:
#   tei (default, legacy /embed) | openai (/v1/embeddings — TEI, llama.cpp,
#   vLLM, Ollama all serve it) | ollama (native /api/embed)
# PAGEINDEX_EMBEDDING_MODEL is sent in the body for openai/ollama backends;
# the tei backend ignores it (model pinned server-side via TEI_MODEL).
# EMBEDDING_DIM must match both the served model and the store's vector(N)
# columns — preflight probes and refuses embed writes on mismatch.
# TLS deploys: PAGEINDEX_EMBEDDING_URL=https://stunnel-tei:443 (compose
# default).
# EMBEDDING_MODEL feeds ONLY the last-resort local-Ollama fallback
# (worker.embedding.local) — it is not the live embedder's model.
EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# (Watched folders are configured in noomesh.yaml `watched_folders.folders`.)

# Tailscale overlay — remote node enrollment via `noo cluster mint-join-key`
# API key: Tailscale admin console → Settings → Keys. Tailnet defaults to the
# API key's own tailnet; script URL defaults to the public OSS repo copy.
NOO_TAILSCALE_API_KEY=
NOO_TAILSCALE_TAILNET=
NOO_BOOTSTRAP_SCRIPT_URL=

# Network binding. Which interface the cluster-plane ports
# publish on: gateway 8080, NATS 4222/6222, node-agent 8081, Grafana 3000,
# node/GPU exporters 9100/9400. Set the *same* value on every node — the
# launcher auto-derives each node's actual address, so there is no per-node IP
# to manage:
#   NOO_BIND_MODE=tailnet   confine to this node's Tailscale IP (auto-detected
#                           via `tailscale ip -4`) — recommended once you're
#                           all-tailnet. NB: reissue gateway/service certs to
#                           include the tailnet IP/MagicDNS name in their SAN
#                           first, or TLS hostname checks over the tailnet fail.
#   NOO_BIND_MODE=local     127.0.0.1 — this machine only (a single box with no tailnet).
#   NOO_BIND_MODE=all       0.0.0.0 (all interfaces) — every network this box is on,
#                           including the LAN or a public interface. Every cluster
#                           port (gateway login, NATS, the mTLS model cache) answers on
#                           whatever this resolves to, so `all` is a decision, not a default.
# `noo onboard` asks and writes the answer; unset or empty still means `all`
# for installs that predate the question.
NOO_BIND_MODE=

# node-agent privilege. true (default) = current behavior. Set
# false to run the node-agent unprivileged (much smaller blast radius); a GPU
# node then needs the nvidia device reservation / /dev/dxg mount, and you should
# front docker.sock with a socket-proxy. Roll out on one node first.
NOO_NODE_AGENT_PRIVILEGED=true

# Node geographic/site label shown in the eval report + dashboard. Set per node
# (e.g. home-lab, us-east). If unset, the launcher derives the Tailscale DERP
# relay region (e.g. derp:iad) as a rough signal; blank falls back to "tailnet".
NODE_LOCATION=

# Secrets backend.
#
#   sops  (default) — decrypts SOPS+age-encrypted secrets/env.sops at
#                     launch. No plaintext secrets at rest. This is what
#                     docs/security/secrets.md describes and what `noo
#                     onboard` configures. Bootstrap:
#                     scripts/noo-secrets-sops-init.sh.
#   env             — an EXPLICIT DOWNGRADE: read this plaintext .env
#                     instead. Every secret then lives unencrypted on disk,
#                     readable by anything that can read the file. Choose it
#                     only for a throwaway dev box, and know that you chose
#                     it.
#
# The default is `sops` so that a new install is never plaintext unless the
# operator chooses it.
#
# See docs/security/secrets.md (setup + rotation runbook).
NOO_SECRETS_BACKEND=sops

# Audit-log integrity. Dedicated HMAC key for the per-row
# tamper-evidence hash. If unset it's derived from NOO_JWT_SECRET (with a
# warning) — set a separate key so a leaked JWT secret can't forge audit hashes.
# Verify with the verify_audit_integrity() helper.
NOO_AUDIT_HMAC_KEY=

# Tenant isolation (cross-user scoping) is UNCONDITIONAL. A data read with no resolved user identity now
# resolves one: on an install with exactly one enabled account that account is
# the identity; with zero or several, the read fails CLOSED (empty memory
# search, no auto-injected KB context). Link every channel sender
# (`noo security users link`) on a multi-account install so its reads resolve.

# Operator identity that TRUSTED internal/system callers act as: the terminal
# `noo inference run` CLI (cluster-HMAC auth) and heartbeat/scheduler tasks.
# Without it, those callers have no user_id, so on a multi-account install
# their memory/RAG context fails closed. Defaults to NOO_ADMIN_USER; set only
# to override. `noo inference run --user <name>` overrides per-call.
NOO_OPERATOR_USER=

# Postgres transport. The server is TLS-only and requires a cluster-CA client
# certificate from every TCP client (docker/postgres/pg_hba.conf); every
# compose DSN carries sslmode=verify-full + sslrootcert + sslcert/sslkey
# itself. These two are only the Python fallback for a DSN that names no
# sslmode (NOO_PG_TLS_REQUIRED=1 refuses to start on one). No opt-out exists.
NOO_PG_SSLMODE=
NOO_PG_TLS_REQUIRED=
# Escape hatch: an explicit address here overrides NOO_BIND_MODE entirely.
NOO_BIND_ADDR=

# Trusted forwarding proxies. Comma-separated
# addresses whose X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP the gateway will trust for the
# rate-limit key AND the audit IP. Loopback is always trusted. Leave empty
# unless a real reverse proxy fronts the gateway.
#
# List the proxy's EXACT address — a proxy container on the gateway's own
# docker network (e.g. "172.20.0.7"). Do NOT list the bridge itself
# ("172.20." or "172.20.0.1"): docker publishes the gateway's port, so every
# remote client arrives FROM the bridge, and a bridge entry would let each one
# choose its own rate-limit bucket and its own IP in the audit log. The
# gateway reads its own routes and REFUSES TO START on such an entry.
#
# Dotted prefixes ending in "." are still accepted for a genuinely separate
# range (e.g. "10.8.0." for a tailnet), just not for the gateway's own.
NOO_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Outbound egress allow-list. Comma-separated domain suffixes an
# outbound fetch may reach (e.g. "api.anthropic.com,duckduckgo.com"). When set,
# any URL whose host isn't listed is refused before connect. Empty = allow any
# public host (private/loopback/link-local are always blocked).
NOO_EGRESS_ALLOWLIST=

# External-inference reranker (optional)
# Deploy the GPU reranker with: COMPOSE_PROFILES=...,embedding  (runs `reranker` + `stunnel-reranker`)
# Rerank needs no configuration: it mesh-routes to whichever node advertises
# the `rerank` capability (gateway -> node-agent /rerank, mTLS + HMAC), and
# falls back to the LLM reranker when no node does. There is no URL to set.
# RERANK_MODEL=BAAI/bge-reranker-base

# ── Operator approvals (agent-guard notifications) ──────────────────
# Where proposal + exec-approval requests get pinged (both optional).
# Google Chat space (admin console → space URL, e.g. spaces/AAQAxxxxxxx):
NOO_APPROVAL_GCHAT_SPACE=
# Telegram chat_id (uses TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN above):
NOO_APPROVAL_TELEGRAM_CHAT=
# Exec-approval mode (auto | approval | strict). POSTURE GATE: the real home
# is the noo_security_posture table (`noo security posture set
# NOO_EXEC_APPROVAL_MODE approval`), which the gateway copies into its env at
# boot. This line is the BOOTSTRAP fallback only — the value used before the
# DB row is read, or when the table is empty. There is no noomesh.yaml tier:
# setting `security.exec_approval_mode` there is a startup refusal.
# Unset here AND in posture = `strict` (HIGH-risk tool calls are denied).
# NOO_EXEC_APPROVAL_MODE=approval

# ── Observability ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PromQL endpoint for noo_metrics_query / the coordinator's metrics tool:
PROMETHEUS_URL=https://prometheus:9090
# Alertmanager for noo_alerts_list (read-only):
# NOO_ALERTMANAGER_URL=https://alertmanager:9093

# ── Postgres replication (warm standby) ──────────────────────────────
# Bind address for the published Postgres port. Set to the controller's
# tailnet IP so only overlay peers (standbys) can reach replication:
# POSTGRES_BIND=100.x.y.z

# ── Rebuild tuning (scripts/noo-mesh-rebuild.sh) ────────────────────
# Docker build-cache floor kept after a successful build (default 20GB):
# NOO_BUILD_CACHE_KEEP=20GB
# Set 1 to SKIP removing inference backends before builds (they are
# removed by default for memory headroom; they auto-restart after):
# NOO_REBUILD_KEEP_BACKENDS=1

# Default SSH user for cluster host operations when SSH_USER is not set (falls back to the current user).
#NOO_SSH_USER_DEFAULT=

# ── Install mode (bootstrap flag) ────────────────────────────────────
# Persisted dev-vs-dist answer from `noo onboard`, read by
# scripts/noo-mesh-start.sh before anything else loads. `dist` makes a
# bare `noo stack start` behave exactly like `--dist` (newest stack-v*
# release, pull-only, no local build); absent or `dev` builds from the
# local source tree — today's behavior. Explicit flags always win:
# `noo stack start --dev` forces a one-off source run without changing
# the mode, `--build` implies dev, `--dist` forces dist.
#NOO_INSTALL_MODE=dev

# ── Image source (dev build vs distribution) ─────────────────────────
# Where the first-party images come from. Leave BOTH unset for normal
# development: the compose files default to noomesh/<img>:latest and
# build from your local source tree.
#
# You normally do not set these by hand — `noo stack start --dist` exports
# them for you and layers docker-compose.dist.yml (which flips pull_policy
# from `build` to `missing`, so nothing compiles). Set them explicitly only
# to pin a permanent distribution install on a non-dev box:
#   NOO_IMAGE_PREFIX=ghcr.io/noomesh
#   NOO_IMAGE_TAG=v0.1.8
# Pulling requires `docker login ghcr.io` with a read:packages PAT — the
# add-on images are in a private registry; the core images are public. See docs/development/release-images.md.
#NOO_IMAGE_PREFIX=
#NOO_IMAGE_TAG=

# ── Add-ons are NOT here ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOO_PRO_ENABLED, NOO_TRELLION_ENABLED, NOO_TRELLION_INTEL_ENABLED and
# NOO_VOICE_ENABLED are RETIRED. `noo stack start` refuses to run if it finds
# any of them in .env, because a stale flag beside a live declaration is how a
# licensed add-on silently changes state on upgrade.
#
# Add-ons are declared once, in noomesh.yaml:
#
#   addons:
#     trellion: true
#     evolve: true
#
# The launcher reads that map for compose profiles and overlays; the gateway
# reads the same mounted file for routing and refusals. `noo onboard --only
# addons` writes it. See docs/development/addons.md.

# ── External MCP door (native Rust, gateway :8080 /mcp/external) ──────
# Lets someone else's agent (their Claude Code, a harness) use the external-tagged
# noomesh tools. Default off.
#
# Enabling the door and authenticating to it are SEPARATE, deliberately.
# The old Python door used a single NOO_MCP_EXTERNAL_TOKEN as both, so it
# could not be opened without also creating a shared credential that ran as
# the operator — unattributed and unrestricted. No door honours that
# variable. This flag only opens the door.
#
# Callers authenticate with a service key bound to a Mesh account. The
# operator creates the account; the account holder mints their own key
# (`service-key issue` has no way to name a user — it mints unbound machine
# keys for /api/external, and the MCP door REFUSES those with a 403):
#   operator:  noo security user create alice --scope read
#   as alice:  noo auth login && noo security service-key self --note laptop
# Her tool calls then run under alice's OWN scopes and KB roles, so a
# read-scope user gets read-scope behaviour here exactly as on the dashboard.
#
# Exposure is separate again: the door listens on the gateway, so reaching it
# from another device means `tailscale serve` or an ACL grant.
#
# This is the BOOTSTRAP fallback. The managed home is security posture
# (`noo security posture set NOO_MCP_EXTERNAL_ENABLED 1`) — DB-authoritative,
# operator-only, audited. Not noomesh.yaml: `noo config set` is reachable
# by any write-scope user, who could then open this door on themselves.
NOO_MCP_EXTERNAL_ENABLED=0

# ── Voice services (STT + TTS) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Deploy the opt-in voice containers: noo-whisper (STT, docker/Dockerfile.whisper)
# and noo-piper (TTS, docker/Dockerfile.piper). ONE switch covers both.
# When 1, `noo stack start/rebuild` activates the "voice" compose profile, so
# the services come back after a restart or host reboot instead of needing a
# manual `docker compose --profile voice up -d whisper piper`. This is
# deployment only — which providers the gateway talks to is NOO_STT_* /
# NOO_TTS_* (defaulting to http://noo-whisper:8000 and http://noo-piper:8000,
# wire format "openai" for both). Deployment is `addons: {voice: true}` in
# noomesh.yaml — see the add-on note above.

# Which whisper model / piper voice the containers preload. Both are STAGED
# by the controller and loaded offline (i105): the containers never download.
# `noo addons enable voice` and `noo upgrade --stack` stage these two; stage
# any other by name with `noo models pull whisper:<size>` (tiny, base, small,
# medium, large-v3, *.en, or an HF CTranslate2 repo id) or
# `noo models pull piper:<voice>` (any id from rhasspy/piper-voices, e.g.
# en_GB-alba-medium). A request for one that is not staged is a 404 naming
# that command.
#WHISPER_MODEL=base
#PIPER_VOICE=en_US-lessac-medium

# Point the gateway at a DIFFERENT TTS provider instead of noo-piper — any
# OpenAI-compatible speech server (OpenAI cloud, LocalAI, speaches,
# kokoro-fastapi, openedai-speech). openedai-speech is also how you keep
# edge-tts voices: edge-tts is a signed WebSocket client, not an HTTP
# service, so it cannot be a provider on its own.
#   NOO_TTS_BACKEND: openai (default wire format) | piper (native /synthesize)
#   NOO_TTS_VOICE:   pin a voice; empty = honour the client's --voice hint
#   NOO_TTS_API_KEY_ENV: the NAME of the env var holding the key — never the
#                        key itself (same rule as NOO_STT_API_KEY_ENV)
#NOO_TTS_URL=https://api.openai.com
#NOO_TTS_BACKEND=openai
#NOO_TTS_VOICE=
#NOO_TTS_API_KEY_ENV=OPENAI_API_KEY
